Chronology of My Implementation
I began by implementing the core model classes in the order recommended by the specification:

Owner – simple fields and validation.

Pet – base class with ID generation, name truncation, attendance tracking, and abstract fee method.

Mammal – extended Pet with sex, vaccination, neutering, and weight validation.

Dog and Cat – added breed validation, dangerous breed logic, indoor cat logic, favourite toy validation.

Bird (abstract) – wingspan validation and canFly flag.

Parrot – vocabulary size conversion using BirdUtility.

Once the model layer was stable, I implemented the OwnerAPI and PetsDayCareAPI, focusing on CRUD operations, reporting methods, counting methods, sorting, and weekly income calculations.

After the APIs were functional, I moved on to the Driver class to handle user input and integrate all components into a working console application.

JUnit 5 test suite – covering all model classes and APIs, achieving high coverage for PetsDayCareAPI.

Finally, I completed the Additional Requirements:

Rabbit class – extending Mammal with new fields (earType, earLengthCm, furColour, favouriteFood) and full validation.

RabbitUtility – validation helpers for earType and furColour.

RabbitTest – JUnit 5 tests covering validation, fee calculation, and defaults.

PetsDayCareAPI updates – added rabbit support to save(), load(), counting, and reporting.

Driver updates – added menu option and input prompts for creating rabbits.

Main Difficulties and How I Solved Them
1. Constructor Mismatches
The biggest challenge was aligning constructor signatures across Pet, Mammal, Bird, Dog, Cat, and Parrot.
Several errors appeared such as:

“Expected X arguments but found Y”

“Bird is abstract; cannot be instantiated”

Missing fields like sex, vaccinated, weight, neutered

I solved this by carefully reviewing each class and ensuring the API and Driver used the correct constructor signatures.

2. PetsDayCareAPI Load/Save Logic
The load() method originally parsed fields incorrectly (wrong indexes, missing mammal fields, incorrect bird/parrot handling).
I rewrote the parsing logic to match the actual constructors and removed attempts to instantiate abstract Bird.

3. Weekly Fee Logic
Dog and Cat do not use numDaysPerWeek, which caused confusion in fee calculations.
I chose Option A: leave Dog and Cat unchanged and accept that their weekly fee depends entirely on numOfDaysAttending().

4. Driver Input Errors
Driver attempted to call ScannerInput.readNextChar(), which did not exist.
I implemented the method myself with proper validation.

5. Test Suite Coverage
Achieving high coverage required writing tests for:

CRUD operations

Reporting methods

Counting methods

Sorting

Validation logic

Weekly fee calculations

I used AI assistance to generate initial drafts, then refined them manually to ensure correctness.

Remaining Bugs or Unfinished Elements
Persistence (save/load) does not fully support all pet types (e.g., Bird is abstract but still saved).

Utilities (DogBreedUtility, CatToyUtility, BirdUtility) are assumed to work correctly but not fully tested.

Driver does not validate all user inputs (e.g., invalid numbers, invalid characters).

Weekly fee logic for Dog and Cat may not reflect real-world behaviour since they default to 0 days unless checked in.

Main Learnings from the Assignment
The importance of consistent constructor design across inheritance hierarchies.

How small mismatches in parameter order or type can cascade into large debugging sessions.

The value of unit testing for catching regressions early.

How abstract classes enforce design constraints (e.g., Bird cannot be instantiated).

The need for careful file parsing and defensive programming when loading data.

How AI tools can accelerate development but still require human oversight.

